{"row":{"id":"fcrn","label":"FcRn","gene":"FCGRT","blurb":"Speed the clearance of pathogenic IgG antibodies."},"trials_total":37,"counts_by_status":{"finished":14,"stopped":3,"advanced":1,"running":19},"counts_by_phase":{"Phase 2":8,"Phase 3":24,"Phase 4":5},"companies":{"argenx":11,"UCB":10,"J&J":5,"Immunovant":3,"Harbour BioMed":2,"AstraZeneca":1,"Shanghai Zhongshan Hospital":1,"Rambam Health Care Campus":1,"Clinique Neuro-Outaouais":1,"University of Colorado, Denver":1},"chain":{"family":"FCGRT · IgG recycling","drugs":["alxn1830","argx-113","batoclimab bi-weekly","efgartigimod","efgartigimod administration","efgartigimod or efgartigimod ph20","efgartigimod ph20","hbm9161","imvt-1402","m281","nipocalimab","rozanolixizumab","rvt-1401","ucb7665"],"approved":{"MG":[{"drug":"efgartigimod alfa","regulator":null,"year":null,"withdrawn":false},{"drug":"nipocalimab","regulator":null,"year":null,"withdrawn":false},{"drug":"rozanolixizumab","regulator":null,"year":null,"withdrawn":false}]},"related_targets":["c5"]},"note":"Showing 20 of 37 trials (stopped first, then running, advanced, finished; newest first). Counts cover all trials."}"}, {"id": "RISK:fcrn:MG", "text": "{"summary":{"median":0.249,"n":12,"typical":0.136},"per_trial":false,"generated_for":"2026-11-11"}"}, {"id": "PMID:42964369", "text": "{"id":"PMID:42964369","title":"Individualized sequencing of targeted immunotherapies in generalized myasthenia gravis across distinct patient profiles.","year":"2026","journal":"Frontiers in neurology","abstract":"Background Targeted immunotherapies have expanded treatment options for acetylcholine receptor antibody-positive generalized myasthenia gravis (gMG), but evidence regarding their sequencing and individualized therapeutic pathways remains limited. Methods We describe seven selected adults with AChR-positive gMG managed through individualized targeted-treatment pathways involving administered and, in some cases, planned sequential therapy. Treatment selection was guided by disease severity, corticosteroid-related risks, presence of thymoma, comorbidities, peripheral venous access and the need for rapid clinical improvement and/or sustained disease management. Results Across the seven cases, FcRn antagonists were used for rapid symptom control, corticosteroid dose reduction and bridging therapy while the appropriate vaccinations required before planned complement inhibition or B-cell depletion therapy were completed. Rituximab was selected as a long-term B-cell-directed immunosuppressive strategy, particularly in recent-onset gMG. Ravulizumab was planned as a long-term, non-steroidal immunosuppressive therapy in thymoma-associated myasthenia gravis and in chronic gMG with symptoms re…"}
{"hit_count":547}"}, {"id": "PMID:42903245", "text": "{"id":"PMID:42903245","title":"The effect of FcRn inhibition on overlapping autoimmune comorbidity in AChR-seropositive generalized myasthenia gravis.","year":"2026","journal":"Frontiers in neurology","abstract":"Introduction Generalized Myasthenia gravis (gMG) is a rare autoimmune disorder of the neuromuscular junction caused by IgG autoantibodies against antigens in the postsynaptic cleft. This study aimed to assess the real-world effectiveness and safety of Efgartigimod (EFG) in AChR-positive gMG with overlapping autoimmune comorbidity. Methods EFG was administered as 4 consecutive weekly infusions or injections. Myasthenia Gravis Activity of Daily Living (MG-ADL) and Myasthenia Gravis quantitative (QMG) scales were administered at baseline and after 4 weeks. Clinical data on autoimmune comorbidities and prednisone use were collected. Results 35 patients (20 women, mean age 53.6 years) received EFG, with a significant and clinical improvement in both MG-ADL and QMG scores. MG-ADL responder rate (MG-ADL reduction≥2) was 79%, while 68% on QMG (QMG reduction≥3); Minimal symptoms expression was reached in 4 patients, with a mean reduction in daily prednisone dose of 8 mg. Overlapping autoimmune comorbidity was reported in 12 gMG patients (34%). gMG patients with Stiff-Person syndrome, type 1 Diabetes and Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome benefited more from EFG with a higher reduction on MG…"}
{"hit_count":547}"}, {"id": "PMID:42931254", "text": "{"id":"PMID:42931254","title":"Targeted Therapies in Generalized Myasthenia Gravis: An Umbrella Review of Evidence Quality and Overlap.","year":"2026","journal":"NeuroSci","abstract":"Generalized myasthenia gravis (gMG) is an antibody-mediated autoimmune disorder in which complement C5 inhibitors and neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn) antagonists have expanded therapeutic options, while rituximab remains used off-label in selected patients. We conducted an umbrella review to critically appraise systematic reviews and evidence syntheses evaluating complement inhibitors and/or FcRn antagonists within a therapeutic framework that also included rituximab. MEDLINE/PubMed, Embase, Epistemonikos, Google Scholar, and supplementary citation sources were searched from 2015 onward, with an updated search on 15 August 2026. Methodological quality was assessed with AMSTAR 2 and primary-study overlap with the corrected covered area (CCA) and GROOVE. Ten reviews/evidence syntheses met eligibility criteria. Complement inhibitors and FcRn antagonists were generally associated with improvements in MG-ADL and QMG, although estimates and rankings varied. AMSTAR 2 rated one review as high confidence, four as low confidence, and five as critically low confidence. Primary-study overlap was very high (CCA 32.59%), with 36 of 45 pairwise comparisons classified as very high. Comparative eviden…"}
{"hit_count":547}"}, {"id": "PMID:42920067", "text": "{"id":"PMID:42920067","title":"Current and Emerging Therapies for Generalized Myasthenia Gravis: Toward Precision Medicine.","year":"2026","journal":"Neurology and therapy","abstract":"The therapeutic landscape of myasthenia gravis (MG) has changed substantially with advances in immunopathogenesis and the introduction of targeted, mechanism-based immunotherapies. For decades, treatment relied primarily on corticosteroids and conventional immunosuppressive agents, which are effective but often limited by delayed onset, long-term toxicity, and incomplete disease control. This review summarizes current and emerging therapies for generalized MG, focusing on mechanisms of action, clinical efficacy, safety, and practical considerations, while also addressing real-world evidence, treatment switching and sequencing, and factors relevant to individualized decision-making. Complement-targeted therapies, including eculizumab, ravulizumab, zilucoplan, gefurulimab, and the emerging RNA interference-based agent cemdisiran, and neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn) inhibitors, including efgartigimod, rozanolixizumab, and nipocalimab, have substantially expanded therapeutic options. B cell-targeted therapies, particularly rituximab and inebilizumab, have also broadened the therapeutic landscape, especially for refractory disease and muscle-specific kinase antibody-positive MG. Emerging a…"}
